{"parentUuid":"5e8448ff-fca6-40df-b995-aab577dcd36e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"acb9bd4fdd02868c2","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01RuG7Jh79mts3NnsNMq7Nhm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015tpyJeoFWru42z8PLTsYx2","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"MIP epsilon optimal gap convergence rate branch and bound last percent time"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7228,"cache_read_input_tokens":13121,"output_tokens":179,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7228},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":179,"cache_read_input_tokens":13121,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7228,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7228,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoyU4Egw785GYu9A5ViM","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c58dc407-8551-4b27-95c3-cbcba78557b0","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:10:57.978Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"c58dc407-8551-4b27-95c3-cbcba78557b0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ac68b9dc-9847-4e6f-8ec2-6b4367414408","agentId":"acb9bd4fdd02868c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015tpyJeoFWru42z8PLTsYx2","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MIP epsilon optimal gap convergence rate branch and bound last percent time\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"What is the MIPGap? – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/articles/8265539575953-What-is-the-MIPGap\"},{\"title\":\"MIP: THEORY AND PRACTICE CLOSING THE GAP\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-0-387-35514-6_2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"13.4 The Optimizer for Mixed-Integer Problems — MOSEK Fusion API for C++ 11.1.11\",\"url\":\"https://docs.mosek.com/latest/cxxfusion/mip-optimizer.html\"},{\"title\":\"MIP 1: Solvers and Branch-and-Bound\",\"url\":\"https://mgkay.github.io/www501F21/5-MIP/5-MIP-1.html\"},{\"title\":\"How is the optimality gap, or MIP gap, calculated? – SimpleRose Knowledge Garden\",\"url\":\"https://support.simplerose.com/hc/en-us/articles/32668122254995-How-is-the-optimality-gap-or-MIP-gap-calculated\"},{\"title\":\"Computational Integer Programming Lecture 8: Branch and Bound Dr. Ted Ralphs\",\"url\":\"https://coral.ise.lehigh.edu/~ted/files/computational-mip/lectures/Lecture8.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"An approximation algorithm for multiobjective mixed-integer convex optimization | Mathematical Methods of Operations Research | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00186-024-00870-3\"},{\"title\":\"Heuristics as warm start for Mixed Integer Programming (MIP) models | Towards Data Science\",\"url\":\"https://towardsdatascience.com/heuristics-as-warm-start-for-mixed-integer-programming-mip-models-9046781dd21f/\"},{\"title\":\"Quick start - Python MIP Documentation - Read the Docs\",\"url\":\"https://python-mip.readthedocs.io/en/latest/quickstart.html\"},{\"title\":\"MIP Models - Gurobi Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.gurobi.com/documentation/9.0/refman/mip_models.html\"}]\n\nI found information related to your search query about MIP (Mixed Integer Programming) epsilon optimal gaps and branch-and-bound convergence.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Epsilon-Optimal Solutions:**\nAn incumbent solution is ε-optimal, meaning that its objective value is within ε (e.g., 0.01%) relative difference from the best possible objective value.\n\n**Gap Convergence and Computational Time:**\nIn branch-and-bound algorithms, solvers may find good feasible solutions early while the current MIP gap is much larger than the allowed tolerance (e.g., current MIP gap of 40% vs an ε of 0.01%), so the rest of the optimization time is spent trying to get the certificate of optimality by pushing the dual bound towards the objective value of the incumbent solution.\n\n**Why Final Percent Takes Time:**\nSmaller gaps lead to marginal solution improvements but require exponentially more effort. This explains the common observation that closing the last percentage points of the MIP gap is computationally expensive relative to the early phase of optimization.\n\n**Practical Search Stopping:**\nIn practice, the search can be stopped when the relative gap falls below a value of 1%, for example.\n\nThe search results indicate that achieving tight epsilon optimality gaps in branch-and-bound algorithms requires disproportionate computational effort due to the exponential nature of the tree search and the difficulty in improving dual bounds toward the final stages of optimization.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"4fc8af06-daa3-4c8f-9e31-f5c09f1086c5","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:11:04.613Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c58dc407-8551-4b27-95c3-cbcba78557b0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"5e8448ff-fca6-40df-b995-aab577dcd36e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ac68b9dc-9847-4e6f-8ec2-6b4367414408","agentId":"acb9bd4fdd02868c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JiQpQBBhAvsSNZEPTn8egU","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PTAS FPTAS theory complexity epsilon convergence rate Vazirani approximation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Polynomial-time Approximation Schemes (PTAS) - Timo Denk's Blog\",\"url\":\"https://blog.timodenk.com/ptas/\"},{\"title\":\"Chapter 18 APPROXIMATION ALGORITHMS Carla P. Gomes\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/gomes/MYPAPERS/gomes-williams05.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Polynomial-time approximation scheme - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial-time_approximation_scheme\"},{\"title\":\"ApproximationAlgorithms\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.yale.edu/homes/aspnes/pinewiki/ApproximationAlgorithms.html\"},{\"title\":\"Structure of Polynomial-Time Approximation | Theory of Computing Systems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00224-011-9366-z\"},{\"title\":\"Fully polynomial-time approximation scheme - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fully_polynomial-time_approximation_scheme\"},{\"title\":\"Polynomial-time Approximation Schemes (PTAS)\",\"url\":\"https://timodenk.com/arxiv/201904-ptas.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Vijay V. Vazirani College of Computing Georgia Institute of Technology\",\"url\":\"https://ics.uci.edu/~vazirani/book.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"FPTAS: The Ultimate Guide to Efficient Approximation\",\"url\":\"https://www.numberanalytics.com/blog/fptas-ultimate-guide-to-efficient-approximation\"},{\"title\":\"Time Approximation Scheme - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/time-approximation-scheme\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comprehensive overview of PTAS and FPTAS theory, complexity, and their relationship to approximation algorithms:\n\n## Core Definitions\n\nA polynomial-time approximation scheme (PTAS) is a type of approximation algorithm for optimization problems that takes an instance of an optimization problem and a parameter ε > 0 and produces a solution that is within a factor 1 + ε of being optimal (or 1 – ε for maximization problems).\n\nA fully polynomial-time approximation scheme (FPTAS) requires the algorithm to be polynomial in both the problem size n and 1/ε.\n\n## Key Differences in Complexity\n\nThe critical distinction between PTAS and FPTAS lies in their runtime behavior regarding the approximation parameter:\n\n- The running time of a PTAS is required to be polynomial in the problem size for every fixed ε, but can be different for different ε; thus an algorithm running in time O(n^(1/ε)) or even O(n^exp(1/ε)) counts as a PTAS.\n\n- The run-time of an FPTAS is polynomial in the problem size and in 1/ε, in contrast to a general PTAS whose run-time might be exponential in 1/ε.\n\nA practical problem with PTAS algorithms is that the exponent of the polynomial could increase dramatically as ε shrinks; one way of addressing this is to define the efficient polynomial-time approximation scheme or EPTAS, in which the running time is required to be O(n^c) for a constant c independent of ε.\n\n## Complexity Hierarchy\n\nUnless P = NP, it holds that FPTAS ⊊ PTAS ⊊ APX.\n\n## Vazirani and Approximation Theory\n\nVazirani (2013) provides a comprehensive overview of approximation algorithms. The Knapsack problem is a classic example: the Knapsack approximation runs in O(n³/ε) time, because the resulting FPTAS is polynomial in both n and 1/ε.\n\n## Applications and Limitations\n\nUnless P = NP, it holds that FPTAS ⊊ PTAS. For instance, any strongly NP-hard optimization problem with a polynomially bounded objective function cannot have an FPTAS unless P=NP.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"825fefc7-614c-4da0-898a-8fa599a74f54","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:11:07.772Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5e8448ff-fca6-40df-b995-aab577dcd36e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
