{"parentUuid":"1dd6e5ab-66cc-4288-80f4-3eb99be63a17","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ac68b9dc-9847-4e6f-8ec2-6b4367414408","agentId":"acb9bd4fdd02868c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0163zP6YyaZywPEzSybQBPfg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"LP relaxation strength integrality gap MIP solver time empirical\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Linear programming relaxation - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_programming_relaxation\"},{\"title\":\"On the Strength of Linear Relaxations in Ordered Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.09166\"},{\"title\":\"On the strength of approximate linear programming relaxations for\",\"url\":\"https://www2.isye.gatech.edu/~atoriello3/bcpalp.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CS787: Advanced Algorithms Lecture 10: LP Relaxation and Rounding\",\"url\":\"https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~shuchi/courses/787-F09/scribe-notes/lec10.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Relaxations and Bounds: Applications to Knapsack Problems\",\"url\":\"https://sofdem.github.io/teach/oro/m2oro-ilp-demassey-notes-lec7-8.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"How to measure the difficulty of a Mixed-Linear Integer Programming (MILP) problem? | ResearchGate\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/post/How_to_measure_the_difficulty_of_a_Mixed-Linear_Integer_Programming_MILP_problem\"},{\"title\":\"Lecture 20: LP Relaxation and Approximation Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://yuanz.web.illinois.edu/teaching/B609fa16/L20.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Is a strong linear relaxation sufficient for good performance of a branch and bound? | ResearchGate\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/post/Is_a_strong_linear_relaxation_sufficient_for_good_performance_of_a_branch_and_bound\"},{\"title\":\"Solve the relaxation of the MIP and then solve the MIP without re-solving the root node relaxation – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/360061377772-Solve-the-relaxation-of-the-MIP-and-then-solve-the-MIP-without-re-solving-the-root-node-relaxation\"},{\"title\":\"Convex Relaxations and Integrality Gaps Eden Chlamtac1 and Madhur Tulsiani2\",\"url\":\"https://home.ttic.edu/~madhurt/Papers/sdpchapter.pdf\"}]\n\nI've found several relevant sources on LP relaxation strength, integrality gap, and their empirical relationships with MIP solver performance. Here's a summary of the key findings:\n\n## LP Relaxation and Integrality Gap Concepts\n\nThe integrality gap is the maximum ratio between the solution quality of the integer program and of its relaxation. Typically, the integrality gap translates into the approximation ratio of an approximation algorithm.\n\n## Empirical Impact on MIP Solver Performance\n\nResearch shows a strong connection between LP relaxation quality and solver performance:\n\nThe quality of a convex relaxation directly impacts the efficiency of algorithms for MILP, particularly in location problems, and the scalability of their application to large-scale instances.\n\nPerformance indicators tracked include CPU time required to optimally solve instances, the MIP gap when not solved to optimality, the number of nodes explored in the branch-and-bound tree, and the gap of the LP relaxation with respect to the best feasible value.\n\n## Practical Observations\n\nA big integrality gap is usually bad, and a good LP model, with a low gap is absolutely crucial for a good performance. However, this is not a guarantee—you can have a good model with a small gap, but if there is a lot of symmetry in your model, it will still be a very hard model to solve.\n\nThe sources demonstrate that while LP relaxation strength is a critical empirical predictor of MIP solver difficulty and solution time, other factors like problem structure and symmetry also play important roles.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a93e50ca-fe70-415e-85ef-294202a36897","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:11:18.075Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1dd6e5ab-66cc-4288-80f4-3eb99be63a17","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"f52d65ee-b912-4dc9-a4d7-c71f5390f20d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ac68b9dc-9847-4e6f-8ec2-6b4367414408","agentId":"acb9bd4fdd02868c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013CMZngQ4wh5tgKuzBq9kq1","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"anytime algorithm competitive ratio convergence profile branch and bound\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Branch and bound - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Branch_and_bound\"},{\"title\":\"The Branch-and-Bound Algorithm in Optimizing Mathematical Programming Models to Achieve Power Grid Observability\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2075-1680/12/11/1040\"},{\"title\":\"A branch and bound algorithm for continuous multiobjective optimization problems using general ordering cones - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0377221725003303\"},{\"title\":\"Branch and Bound Methods Stephen Boyd, Arpita Ghosh, and Alessandro Magnani\",\"url\":\"https://web.stanford.edu/class/ee392o/bb.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Anytime Algorithms for Multi-Armed Bandit Problems Robert Kleinberg∗ 1\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~rdk/papers/anytime.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Branch-and-bound algorithm for a competitive facility location problem | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/257153572_Branch-and-bound_algorithm_for_a_competitive_facility_location_problem\"},{\"title\":\"A Branch and Bound Algorithm for Multiobjective Optimization Problems Using General Ordering Cones\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.10500v1\"},{\"title\":\"Competitive Ratio - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/competitive-ratio\"},{\"title\":\"Branch and Bound Algorithm - GeeksforGeeks\",\"url\":\"https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dsa/branch-and-bound-algorithm/\"},{\"title\":\"A multi-queue branch-and-bound algorithm for anytime optimal search with biological applications - PubMed\",\"url\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11791226/\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about these topics:\n\n## Branch and Bound\n\nBranch-and-bound (BB, B&B, or BnB) is a method for solving optimization problems by breaking them down into smaller subproblems and using a bounding function to eliminate subproblems that cannot contain the optimal solution. A branch-and-bound algorithm systematically enumerates candidate solutions through state-space search by exploring branches of a tree representing subsets of the solution set, and before enumerating candidate solutions of a branch, it checks the branch against upper and lower estimated bounds on the optimal solution, discarding it if it cannot produce a better solution than the best one found so far.\n\n## Convergence in Branch and Bound\n\nRegarding convergence, an algorithm produces an iterative process that is fully convergent when the iteration run approaches the objective function evaluation nearer and nearer to a desired solution, and branch-and-bound algorithms constantly measure the difference between the upper and lower bounds to be less than a default value, which is a specified tolerance.\n\n## Anytime Algorithms\n\nThe search results include a relevant paper on anytime algorithms. A multi-queue variant of branch-and-bound search combines anytime and optimal search behavior. While the search results don't provide extensive detail on competitive ratios in the context of anytime algorithms specifically, competitive ratio is defined as the ratio between the total busy time of an algorithm and the total busy time of the optimal solution for a given computational problem, and it helps evaluate the performance of algorithms in terms of efficiency and competitiveness.\n\nThe combination of these concepts—using branch-and-bound as an anytime algorithm while tracking competitive ratio and convergence profile—represents an approach to optimization that provides intermediate solutions while working toward optimal ones.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"b6a44352-e6c7-410f-9dce-d2e3da8d2969","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:11:18.127Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f52d65ee-b912-4dc9-a4d7-c71f5390f20d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"c0a5956c-4967-4f10-90d2-fd2c454acb8d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ac68b9dc-9847-4e6f-8ec2-6b4367414408","agentId":"acb9bd4fdd02868c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018hnvuv6ggBw3Jj5HQAFooh","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT OR-Tools convergence gap closing time benchmark MIPLIB\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarking your Model - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/08_benchmarking.html\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT claims to find optimal solution to a MILP\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/4fcAuWiIUFA\"},{\"title\":\"Basic Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"Any comparison of MIP and CP-SAT solvers? why MIP solver is not finding better solutions? · Issue #2169 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2169\"}]\n\nI found some relevant results about CP-SAT, OR-Tools, and benchmarking with MIPLIB. Here are the key findings:\n\n## CP-SAT Solver Overview\n\nThe CP-SAT solver is designed for integer programming problems and is generally faster than MPSolver.\n\n## Performance on Integer Programming\n\nFor Linear Integer Programming, CP-SAT only solves pure ILP problems and is better than all open source solvers, closing in on the best commercial solvers, but not competitive on average.\n\n## Benchmarking with MIPLIB\n\nThe MIPLIB collection stands as a scientific contribution in its own right as a notable example of a high-quality benchmark instance set. Split plots show runtime for solved instances and optimality gap for those that exceed the time limit, allowing for a unified view of scalability, which is relevant for analyzing convergence and gap closing performance on benchmark problems.\n\n## Gap and Convergence Parameters\n\nIn addition to limiting runtime, you can specify acceptable solution quality using absolute_gap_limit and relative_gap_limit. The absolute limit stops the solver when the solution is within a specified value of the bound. The relative limit stops the solver when the objective value is within a specified ratio of the bound.\n\nThe search results provide general information about CP-SAT's capabilities and benchmarking approaches, but don't contain specific benchmark results comparing CP-SAT's convergence gap closing times against other solvers on MIPLIB instances. If you're looking for specific performance metrics or research papers with detailed benchmarks, you may need to check the OR-Tools GitHub repository or academic papers directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e5ef12ef-c3f4-47fb-a560-1c2ff5bac496","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:11:18.851Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c0a5956c-4967-4f10-90d2-fd2c454acb8d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"