{"parentUuid":"e21090c5-cd62-473a-8187-a422b7d5e0d3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ac68b9dc-9847-4e6f-8ec2-6b4367414408","agentId":"acb9bd4fdd02868c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019vTNw64QiHngnuAg1xeC9w","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"primal dual integral\" anytime MIP performance metric definition\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Measuring the impact of primal heuristics - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167637713001181\"},{\"title\":\"C H A P T E R 4 THE PRIMAL-DUAL METHOD FOR APPROXIMATION ALGORITHMS AND\",\"url\":\"https://math.mit.edu/~goemans/PAPERS/book-ch4.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Playing with Duality: An Overview of Recent Primal-Dual ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1406.5429\"},{\"title\":\"The confined primal integral\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/7910.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Measuring the Impact of Primal Heuristics Timo Berthold Zuse Institute Berlin\",\"url\":\"https://www.zib.de/userpage//berthold/Berthold_Heuristics.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Duality in MIP Generating Dual Price Functions Using Branch-and-Cut\",\"url\":\"https://ep.liu.se/ecp/014/005/ecp014005.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Measuring the impact of primal heuristics | Operations Research Letters\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1016/j.orl.2013.08.007\"},{\"title\":\"Primal-dual programs for the constrained optimal impulse control: discounted model\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2504.18387\"},{\"title\":\"How well can Primal-Dual and Local-Ratio algorithms perform? ∗ Allan Borodin\",\"url\":\"https://www.ias.edu/sites/default/files/math/csdm/06-07/aborodin_how_well_can_primal_dual_and_local_ratio.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Learn How NVIDIA cuOpt Accelerates Mixed Integer Optimization using Primal Heuristics | NVIDIA Technical Blog\",\"url\":\"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/learn-how-nvidia-cuopt-accelerates-mixed-integer-optimization-using-primal-heuristics\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I can provide you with information about the primal integral as an anytime MIP performance metric:\n\n## Primal Integral Definition\n\nThe \"primal integral\" is a performance measure that depends on the quality of solutions and on the time when they are found. It was introduced to address limitations in classical performance measures for evaluating primal heuristics in Mixed Integer Programming (MIP) solvers.\n\n## Why It Was Developed\n\nIn modern MIP solvers, primal heuristics play a key role in finding high-quality solutions, but classical performance measures reflect the impact of primal heuristics on the overall solving process badly. The traditional metrics like \"time to first solution\" and \"time to optimal solution\" have significant weaknesses for evaluating heuristic performance.\n\n## Key Characteristics\n\nThe primal integral is less prone, though not immune, to common weaknesses of standard performance measures, notably the dependence on an (arbitrarily chosen) time limit. According to the research, the primal integral favors finding good solutions early, considers each update of incumbent, and represents the expected quality of the incumbent if stopped arbitrarily.\n\n## Dual Integral Extension\n\nA dual integral can be defined by considering a dual gap function between the current and a best known dual bound.\n\nThe primal integral has become an important metric in evaluating MIP solver performance, particularly for assessing the effectiveness of heuristic components.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"52923a68-1c3b-4363-a787-85d3e2cd2591","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:11:32.219Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e21090c5-cd62-473a-8187-a422b7d5e0d3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"bd9e22d6-721a-40e4-9a5e-e3ca8c49840c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ac68b9dc-9847-4e6f-8ec2-6b4367414408","agentId":"acb9bd4fdd02868c2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MdbBYEMTSQSSd6hLmzCJie","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MIP gap closing exponential time empirical study \"5%\" \"1%\" benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"MIP: Theory and Practice — Closing the Gap | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-0-387-35514-6_2\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] MIP: Theory and Practice - Closing the Gap | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/MIP:-Theory-and-Practice-Closing-the-Gap-Bixby-Fenelon/a8096c0fd3aaa1884e0b2dce37633763b770f494\"},{\"title\":\"MIP* = RE – Communications of the ACM\",\"url\":\"https://cacm.acm.org/research/mip-re/\"},{\"title\":\"MIP: THEORY AND PRACTICE CLOSING THE GAP\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-0-387-35514-6_2.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"MIP: Theory And Practice - Closing The Gap | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/2410330_MIP_Theory_And_Practice_-_Closing_The_Gap\"},{\"title\":\"Improving solving (gap closing) time – Gurobi Help Center\",\"url\":\"https://support.gurobi.com/hc/en-us/community/posts/15967934995217-Improving-solving-gap-closing-time\"},{\"title\":\"The Computational Advantage of MIP^∗ Vanishes in the Presence of Noise\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CCC.2024.30\"},{\"title\":\"Microdata-based output gap estimation using business tendency surveys - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S016518892500034X\"},{\"title\":\"MIPGap - Gurobi Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.gurobi.com/documentation/9.5/refman/mipgap2.html\"},{\"title\":\"Gap-preserving reductions and RE-completeness of independent set games\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2505.05253v1\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found references to research on Mixed Integer Programming (MIP) gap closing, but the results did not return a specific empirical study matching all your query terms (\"5%\" and \"1%\" with exponential time analysis). \n\nThe most relevant result was a publication titled \"MIP: Theory and Practice — Closing the Gap\", which appears to be a foundational work by Bixby and colleagues on bridging theory and practice in mixed integer programming.\n\nThe search also returned practical discussions about MIP gap closing (for instance, concerning solving time and gap reduction with MIP solvers), but not a specific benchmark study with the particular percentage thresholds you mentioned.\n\nTo find the specific empirical study you're looking for that analyzes exponential time behavior for closing MIP gaps to specific thresholds like 5% and 1%, you might want to try:\n- Searching academic databases like Google Scholar or arXiv directly\n- Refining your search with author names if you know them\n- Checking MIPLIB (Mixed Integer Programming Library) documentation, which often includes empirical performance studies\n\nWould you like me to search for more specific information, such as recent MIPLIB benchmarks or papers focusing on branch-and-bound computational complexity?\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"850840bd-6b97-4690-abc2-75f60c78c39f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:11:32.266Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bd9e22d6-721a-40e4-9a5e-e3ca8c49840c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
